<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1914 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Furusato</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">故郷</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Furusato</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese school song of 1914 about someone far from home who misses the hills and fields where they grew up.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Tatsuyuki Takano</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Teiichi Okano</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Japanese schoolchildren</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1914</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Takano Tatsuyuki, music by Okano Teiichi (published 1914; both died in the 1940s))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Furusato&quot; (&quot;old home&quot; or &quot;hometown&quot;) is a Japanese children's song of 1914, with music by Teiichi Okano and words by Tatsuyuki Takano. Takano's own hometown was Nakano, in Nagano, but the words do not seem to describe any particular place.</p><p>For decades the song was sung without its makers' names: its composer and writer were unknown until the 1970s. Since 1992 the names of both Teiichi Okano and Tatsuyuki Takano have been printed with it in Japanese music textbooks. The Japanese government has designated it as one of the children's songs to be taught in the public schools, and it was included in the collection Nihon no Uta Hyakusen.</p><p>The song was played at the closing ceremony of the 1998 Winter Olympics in Nagano. Plácido Domingo sang it in Japanese at a concert in NHK Hall on 10 April 2011. In 2014, for its hundredth anniversary, a children's chorus with orchestra performed it at the Saito Kinen Festival Matsumoto in Nagano, under the direction of Seiji Ozawa. An English version by Greg Irwin was published on the album &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s Best Loved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of the Season&quot; in 1998 and has also been sung by Lexi Walker.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The song speaks in the voice of someone working in a distant land who longs for the hills and fields of a childhood home. Its mood is quiet and wistful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Japanese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">兎追いし 彼の山</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Usagi oishi kano yama</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That mountain where I chased hares,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">小鮒釣りし 彼の川</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">kobuna tsurishi kano kawa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that river where I fished for little crucian carp:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">夢は今も 巡りて</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">yume wa ima mo megurite</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>even now my dreams go round to them,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">忘れ難き 故郷</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">wasuregataki furusato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my old home, hard to forget.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">如何にいます 父母</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Ika ni imasu chichi haha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How are you, father and mother?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">恙無しや 友がき</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">tsutsuganashi ya tomogaki</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are you without harm, my friends?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">雨に風に つけても</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">ame ni kaze ni tsukete mo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the rain, in the wind, at every turn</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">思い出づる 故郷</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">omoiizuru furusato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I call to mind my old home.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">志を 果たして</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">Kokorozashi o hatashite</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When I have fulfilled my purpose,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">いつの日にか 帰らん</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">itsu no hi ni ka kaeran</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on some day I shall return:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">山は青き 故郷</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">yama wa aoki furusato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my old home where the mountains are green,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">水は清き 故郷</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ja-Latn">mizu wa kiyoki furusato</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my old home where the waters are clear.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Takano Tatsuyuki (1914); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Furusato_(children's_song)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (modified Hepburn).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'oishi' is the classical past form, 'chased' (often misheard by children as 'oishii', delicious).</li><li>line 2.1: 'imasu' is an old honorific 'to be'; 'ika ni imasu', how are (you), respectfully.</li><li>line 2.2: 'tsutsuganashi', without illness or trouble; 'tomogaki', an old word for friends.</li><li>line 2.3: 'tsukete mo', literally 'attaching (my thoughts) even to': whenever rain or wind comes.</li><li>line 3.3: 'aoki' (青き) covers both blue and green; the mountains' green is meant.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
